The reason your GitHub Copilot Agent is struggling with the PowerShell scripts—but succeeds with cloning and building—comes down to how those processes output information.

Cloning (git clone) and building (like dotnet build or npm run build) are synchronous, blocking commands that stream standard output (stdout) continuously until they finish with an explicit exit code. Copilot can easily track their lifecycles.

Large PowerShell scripts, however, often spin up asynchronous background jobs, decoupled processes, or suppress their progress streams, leaving the agent's execution context waiting in a silent limbo. Because the agent doesn't receive a definitive "I am done" signal, it assumes it should stop tracking, forcing you to manually ask for status updates.

To fix this, you need to change how the PowerShell script executes or how the agent orchestrates the step. Here are the best ways to solve this.

Solution 1: Force PowerShell to Block and Report (Recommended)
If your PowerShell script is starting other processes internally (like calling an .exe or an installer), PowerShell might be spawning them asynchronously and returning control to the agent immediately, making the agent think the script finished.

You must force the script to wait for all internal processes to finish before exiting.

Wrap internal commands with Start-Process -Wait
Instead of calling a tool directly inside your script like msiexec.exe /i setup.msi, use:

PowerShell
Start-Process "msiexec.exe" -ArgumentList "/i setup.msi /quiet" -NoNewWindow -Wait
Force Background Jobs to Block
If your script uses Start-Job or thread pools, add a blocking waiter at the very bottom of your script so it doesn't return an exit code to Copilot prematurely:

PowerShell
# At the end of your big script, force it to wait for all background tasks
Get-Job | Wait-Job
Solution 2: Implement an Active "Heartbeat" or Progress Loop
Agents rely heavily on stdout lines to know a process is alive. If a PowerShell script takes 10 minutes to run and prints nothing for 9 of those minutes, the Agent's polling mechanism may timeout or lose track.

Add an explicit progress loop inside your script that writes to the host at regular intervals so Copilot has a continuous stream of data to poll.

PowerShell
Write-Host "Starting long-running database migration..."
# Your heavy logic here

# If you are looping through items, add a heartbeat:
foreach ($task in $bigTasks) {
    # Execute task logic...
    
    // Explicitly print status so the Agent sees active polling data
    Write-Host "[STATUS UPDATE] Completed task: $($task.Name) at $(Get-Date)"
}
Write-Host "Script Execution Completed Successfully."
Solution 3: Change the Agent's Orchestration (The Status-File Trick)
If the script is fundamentally too large and unpredictable to run in a single synchronous window, you should change how the Agent tracks it by using a State file. This moves the polling responsibility from the Agent's internals to an explicit script rule.

Modify your agent's instructions (or the script wrapper) to do this 3-step loop:

Start step: The agent triggers the PowerShell script, which immediately writes Status=Running to a local status.txt file and runs its main logic in the background.

Polling step: Instead of relying on Copilot's built-in process tracker, instruct your Agent to execute a loop command that checks that file every 30 seconds.

Completion step: When the script finishes, it changes the text file to Status=Completed.

The Agent's Polling Command (Pseudo-code for the Agent step):
PowerShell
# Tell the agent to execute this wrapper to monitor the script
Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList "-File C:\Scripts\BigScript.ps1" -NoNewWindow
do {
    Start-Sleep -Seconds 30
    $status = Get-Content -Path "C:\Scripts\status.txt" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    Write-Host "Current Agent Tracking Status: $status"
} while ($status -eq "Running")
Summary Strategy
Check your script internals: Are you missing -Wait flags on external commands called inside the script? If yes, use Solution 1.

Is the script silent for long periods? If yes, use Solution 2 to spray Write-Host checkpoints so the Copilot Agent stream doesn't assume the process went dead.

Is the script massive (over 10-15 minutes)? Use Solution 3 to explicitly write status states to a text file that the agent can intentionally poll via basic commands.
